: 10091.4, "nodata_rule": "a sample is no-data, and never enters a statistic, when its band is missing or NaN, equals the model's nodata_value, lies outside the band's declared regression range, or carries no class on a classification band"}}, "seq": 13, "t": 259.239, "seconds": 196.741, "call_seq": 12}
{"type": "final", "turn": 6, "content": "\n\nBoth 2025 PA Karst predictions are complete, and I compared them cell-by-cell on a grid over their shared extent (~10,091 km\u00b2). Key finding: **they predict different quantities**, so much of the \"difference\" is scale, not disagreement.\n\n| | KarstBinary | KarstNumber |\n|---|---|---|\n| Band | `sample_karst_score` | `sample_number` |\n| Type | regression, value per pixel | regression, value per pixel |\n| Declared range | 0.0 \u2013 1.0 | 0.2 \u2013 1.2 |\n| Mean over AOI | ~0.10 | ~0.69 |\n\nWhere they differ / how they relate:\n- **Correlation across cells: 0.497** (moderate, not strong) \u2014 the two maps do not follow each other's spatial pattern tightly; regions one rates high are not consistently the same as regions the other rates high.\n- Agreement within \u00b10.1: **0% of sampled cells**. But the tool flags that this is misleading \u2014 the bands measure different properties on different scales, so the mean gap (~0.59), RMSE (0.63), and 0% agreement describe *different units*, not one map being \"higher\" or better. Only the correlation is a fair cross-model number.\n- 53 of 288 sampled cells were no-data in one or both maps and dropped.\n\nWhich is right?\n- **Not answerable from these results alone.** There is no ground-truth layer in this project, so neither model's output can be scored for accuracy \u2014 the comparison above is model-vs-model agreement only. Neither band is a confidence or class probability; both are per-pixel values.\n- To actually decide, we need labels: I can run a label-sample design on one (or both) maps (`plan_label_sample` \u2192 you mark N windows \u2192 `estimate_map_error` gives each map an honest error rate), or compare them against any reference karst dataset you have. That would produce per-map accuracy instead of guesses.\n\nWant me to set up the labeled error check on the KarstBinary score, the KarstNumber layer, or both?", "seq": 14, "t": 273.745, "seconds": 14.506}
